The buggy method is `org.jfree.data.time.TimePeriodValues.updateBounds(org.jfree.data.time.TimePeriod, int)`.

The failure is in the `maxMiddleIndex` maintenance logic. In [TimePeriodValues](<PRIVATE_URL>) lines 299-304, the code computes the comparison baseline for `maxMiddleIndex` from `this.minMiddleIndex`:

```java
long s = getDataItem(this.minMiddleIndex).getPeriod().getStart().getTime();
long e = getDataItem(this.minMiddleIndex).getPeriod().getEnd().getTime();
long maxMiddle = s + (e - s) / 2;
```

That should use `this.maxMiddleIndex`, not `this.minMiddleIndex`. Because of that, any newly added item whose middle is greater than the minimum middle can incorrectly replace the true maximum-middle item.

Dynamic verification confirmed the bad branch: at runtime, `minMiddleIndex` and `maxMiddleIndex` had already diverged, but the method still used `minMiddleIndex` as the baseline when deciding whether to update `maxMiddleIndex`. That makes `getMaxMiddleIndex()` return `3` in the test instead of the correct `1`.

